375, -0.0067291259765625, 0.008941650390625, 0.03546142578125, 0.025177001953125, 0.0377197265625, 0.01268768310546875, 0.0087127685546875, -0.00206756591796875, -0.03338623046875, 0.0009360313415527344, 0.00421905517578125, -0.003055572509765625, -0.031768798828125, -0.0007796287536621094, 0.0139007568359375, -0.01375579833984375, -0.0194091796875, -0.00274658203125, -0.0117034912109375, 0.0195770263671875, -0.0185089111328125, -0.0237274169921875, -0.0186920166015625, -0.010833740234375, 0.00894927978515625, -0.0279388427734375, 0.0281219482421875, -0.018890380859375, 0.026336669921875, 0.004119873046875, -0.04888916015625, -0.0084228515625, 0.0118255615234375, 0.00997161865234375, -0.039581298828125, -0.0008215904235839844, 0.026397705078125, -0.0137176513671875, 0.0072174072265625, -0.0226593017578125, -0.00409698486328125, 0.027374267578125, -0.01346588134765625, 0.026641845703125, 0.0040130615234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has begun to investigate the use of Geometric Algebra (GA) in the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems\u2014most notably in the study of electrical circuits (see, e.g., [1], [2], [3]). In these applications, transforming the ordinary differential equations (ODEs) that describe system behavior from the time (real) domain to the Laplace domain is a fundamental technique, facilitating analysis and design. While standard Laplace transforms are well established in real and complex analysis, and have been extended to certain hypercomplex algebras such as quaternions and Clifford algebras [4], [5], there has been limited attention to a rigorous definition and treatment of the Laplace transform within the general framework of Geometric Algebra.\n\nThe gap addressed by our work is the absence of a comprehensive Laplace transform definition tailored for general geometric algebras, particularly where previous extensions to hypercomplex algebras (e.g., Clifford, quaternionic, and dual number algebras) do not account for the broader structure and multivector nature of GA. Our approach provides a constructive definition of the Laplace transform in GA, along with fundamental properties relevant to the analysis of continuous-time dynamical systems modeled using geometric algebraic methods. The focus here is on continuous systems, in line with common treatments of electrical circuits and related physical systems.\n\nWe note that our definition and the corresponding properties are currently applicable to geometric algebras with signature less than or equal to 5. This limitation arises due to the increased computational complexity and algebraic subtleties that occur as the algebra's dimension grows, which impact both the practical computation of the transform and the structural properties required for well-defined Laplace-domain representations. As such, our results are immediately useful for physical applications\u2014such as three-dimensional space (signature (3,0)) and spacetime formulations (e.g., signature (1,3))\u2014where the geometric algebras in use fall within this bound.\n\n[1] Doran, C., & Lasenby, A. (2003). Geometric Algebra for Physicists. Cambridge University Press.\n[2] Hitzer, E., & Sangwine, S. J. (Eds.). (2013). Quaternion and Clifford Fourier Transforms and Wavelets. Springer.\n[3] Lasenby, J., Lasenby, A., & Doran, C. (2000). A Unified Mathematical Language for Physics and Engineering in the 21st Century. Phil. Trans. R. Soc. Lond. A 358, 21-39.\n[4] Kou, K. I., & Liu, L. (2007). Quaternionic Laplace transforms and their applications. Mathematical Methods in the Applied Sciences, 30(15), 1785\u20131797.\n[5] Sobczyk, G. (1995). The Hyperbolic Number Plane. The College Mathematics Journal, 26(4), 268\u2013280."}}